<h1>Case Packing &amp; Case Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Case packing conveyor systems integrate case erectors, product collating conveyors, case packers, case sealers, and case transport conveyors into a complete end-of-line packaging operation. The conveyor system connects each machine, meters product flow, buffers between operations, and transports finished cases to <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">palletizing</a>. The engineering challenge is less about any one OEM and more about how the infeed, buffering, sealing, and downstream transport sections work together without starving or blocking the line.</p>
<h2 id="erector-heading">Case Erectors</h2>
<p>Case erectors take flat (knocked-down) corrugated blanks from a magazine, open them into box shape, fold and seal the bottom flaps, and discharge an empty, open-top case onto a conveyor. The erected case then travels to the case packer for product loading.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/case-wexxar-wf30-case-erector.webp" width="960" height="720" alt="A guarded case erector with a control panel and stack light, a corrugated case visible inside, and a flexible roller conveyor beside it." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A Wexxar WF30 fully automatic case erector with a corrugated case in the forming section and a flexible roller conveyor at one end. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.wexxar.com/products/fully-automatic-case-former-case-erector-wf30/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wexxar Bel</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<p>Two erector types serve different speed and flexibility requirements:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Semi-automatic erectors:</strong> An operator feeds blanks and assists with opening or folding, and the machine seals the bottom. Rate is set by operator pace. Suits low-volume lines and frequent size changes. Cost is driven mainly by the sealing method and size range.</li>
<li><strong>Fully automatic erectors:</strong> Vacuum cups pull blanks from the magazine, then the machine opens, folds, and seals the case with no operator in the cycle. For reference, Wexxar rates its <a href="https://www.wexxar.com/products/fully-automatic-case-former-case-erector-wf30/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">WF30 fully automatic case former</a> at up to 30 cases per minute (CPM). Cost is driven by speed, servo content, and how many case sizes the machine must change over to.</li>
</ul>
<p>The conveyor between case erector and case packer is typically a short section of flat belt or roller conveyor with photoeye sensors. The sensor signals the erector to stop when accumulated cases reach a set count, preventing case buildup that jams the packer infeed. Case spacing on this conveyor matches the packer's loading cycle.</p>
<h2 id="packing-heading">Case Packing Methods</h2>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/case-douglas-top-load-pouch-packing.webp" width="1000" height="668" alt="Four vacuum cup heads lift printed pouches from carriers on an infeed track beside a row of open corrugated cases held by orange flap guides." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Top-load case packing: vacuum cup heads pick pouches from the infeed and lower them into open cases held alongside. Orange guides hold the case flaps open. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.douglas-machine.com/products-solutions/case-and-tray-packers/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Douglas</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h3>Drop-Pack (Vertical) Case Packing</h3>
<p>Drop-pack case packers collate product on a conveyor above the case, then drop the product group vertically into an open-top case positioned below. The product collating conveyor uses <a href="/conveyor-functions/collating-laning/">laning and grouping</a> mechanisms to form the case pattern (e.g., 4x3 or 6x4). A servo-driven bucket or flight system partitions product into groups. The case indexes under the loading station, the loading head descends to place product, and the loaded case advances to sealing.</p>
<p>Drop-pack suits bottles, cans, jars, and rigid containers that withstand the vertical drop without damage. Cushioned landing mechanisms (foam pads, air cylinders) soften the drop for fragile items.</p>
<h3>Wrap-Around Case Packing</h3>
<p>Wrap-around packers form the case around a collated product group. A flat corrugated blank wraps around the product, bottom first, then sides, then top, creating a tightly fitting case with minimal void space. The wrapping motion uses mechanical plows and servo-controlled folding arms.</p>
<p>Wrap-around packing uses less corrugated material than pre-erected cases (no overlapping flaps). The tighter fit protects product better during shipping and reduces dunnage costs. However, products must be stable enough to hold their collated pattern during the wrapping motion. Wrap-around suits multipacks of bottles, cans, cartons, and pouches.</p>
<h3>Robotic Case Packing</h3>
<p><a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/case/robotic-case-packing/">Robotic case packers</a> use delta robots, articulated arms, or gantry systems to pick products from a conveyor and place them into cases. Robotic packing handles the widest range of product types, sizes, and case patterns, recipe-based changeover adjusts pick patterns, place coordinates, and speed. See our dedicated <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/case/robotic-case-packing/">robotic case packing guide</a> for detailed coverage.</p>
<h3>Side-Load Case Packing</h3>
<p>Side-load packers push product horizontally into an open-end case. A collating conveyor groups products, and a pusher mechanism slides the group into the case from the side. Side-load suits cartons, trays, and flat packages. The product conveyor runs parallel to the case conveyor, and the loading mechanism transfers product at right angles.</p>
<h2 id="collate-heading">Product Collating Conveyors</h2>
<p>Product collating conveyors arrange individual products or packages into the case pattern before loading. A case holding 24 bottles in a 4x6 pattern needs the collating conveyor to form 4 lanes of 6 bottles each, precisely spaced and aligned. This is the critical conveyor between the primary packaging line and the case packer.</p>
<p>Collating methods include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Lane dividers:</strong> Guide rails split a single product stream into multiple parallel lanes. Products fill lanes sequentially until the case count is reached. Simple and low cost. Limited to uniform, round products. See <a href="/conveyor-functions/collating-laning/">collating and laning</a> for details.</li>
<li><strong>Servo-driven grouping:</strong> Servo belts with flights or pushers separate and group products at precise intervals. Handles variable product sizes through recipe changes. Higher cost but maximum flexibility.</li>
<li><strong>Robot-based collating:</strong> Delta robots pick individual products and place them into the case pattern on a staging conveyor. Handles mixed products, random orientation, and complex patterns. Highest cost and the most flexibility.</li>
</ul>
<p>The collating conveyor must match the case packer's cycle rate. If the packer loads one case every 3 seconds (20 CPM), the collating conveyor must form a complete pattern every 3 seconds. <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">Accumulation zones</a> upstream buffer product when the packer stops for case changeover or jam clearing.</p>
<h2 id="sealer-heading">Case Sealers</h2>
<p>Case sealers close and seal loaded cases using tape, hot-melt glue, or staples. The sealer integrates with the case conveyor, the loaded case enters the sealer on powered rollers or belt, the top flaps close (gravity, plows, or pneumatic arms), adhesive applies, and the sealed case exits.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tape sealers:</strong> Apply pressure-sensitive tape to the top and bottom center seam. Uniform-size sealers (fixed width and height) are the simplest and least costly. Random-size sealers adjust automatically to each case, which adds sensors and mechanics but removes manual changeover.</li>
<li><strong>Hot-melt sealers:</strong> Apply hot-melt adhesive to the flaps and compress them. Gives a tamper-evident seal and a cleaner appearance than tape. Some retail customers specify glued cases, so confirm customer packaging requirements before choosing. Cost is driven by the glue system, compression section, and heated hoses.</li>
<li><strong>Staple closers:</strong> Wire stitching through corrugated flaps. Used for heavy-duty cases (meat, produce) where tape adhesion can be unreliable in cold or wet conditions.</li>
</ul>
<p>Case sealer throughput must match or exceed the case packer output. A 30 CPM case packer needs a sealer rated for at least 30 CPM. Photoeye sensors at the sealer infeed detect case arrival and trigger the sealing cycle. Tape cutting blades, drive belts, and flap folders create cut and pinch hazards that <a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.212" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OSHA 1910.212</a> requires guarding against.</p>
<h2 id="transport-heading">Case Transport Conveyors</h2>
<p>Case transport conveyors move sealed cases from the sealer to palletizing, labeling, checkweighing, or shipping. Case weights range from light consumer goods to heavy industrial parts, so conveyor type selection depends on the heaviest case, the rate, and routing complexity. Design, guarding, and emergency stops on these conveyors fall under <a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/b20-1-safety-standard-conveyors-related-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASME B20.1</a>.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong><a href="/conveyor-types/live-roller-conveyors/">Belt-driven live roller</a>:</strong> Common for light and moderate case loads. O-ring or poly-V belts drive the rollers. Quiet, and needs less maintenance than chain drives.</li>
<li><strong><a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">Chain-driven live roller (CDLR)</a>:</strong> For heavy cases. Roller chain drives each roller. Handles impact loading from case packers and accumulation pressure from palletizer staging.</li>
<li><strong><a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR (motor-driven roller)</a>:</strong> Zero-pressure accumulation for case staging before palletizer. Each zone independently controlled. Energy-efficient, zones run only when product is present. Preferred for modern <a href="/industries/warehouse-conveyors/">warehouse and distribution</a> systems.</li>
</ul>
<p>Routing conveyors use <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">right-angle transfers</a>, diverters, and merge conveyors to direct cases from multiple packing lines to a central palletizer or shipping area. For budgeting, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a>.</p>
